\ ======================================================================
\ 04 — generics, and what staging means.
\
\ THE CLAIM (D01 §3.2, D-02, D-83): a generic is not a second mechanism. It is
\ the SAME `specialize` that runs the Dictionary in demo 03, run at
\ elaboration time instead of at run time. So a generic call leaves no name,
\ no dictionary entry, no lookup and no type argument in the compiled program
\ — the code that is there is the code for the types you called it at, and
\ `locate` will show you.
\ ======================================================================

\ --- Declaring one. Type parameters go in `[…]` and appear as `#T`.
\ A generic MUST write its signature: inference never generalises, so there
\ would be nothing to generalise from.

define twice[#T] ( #T -- #T #T ) { dup }

5 twice show print " " print show print "\n" print

"hi" twice print print "\n" print

\ --- The types come from the stack, and each call gets its own copy, spliced
\ into the caller. `quad` is written once over `#T` and calls `twice` three
\ times; `q` calls it at `i64`. Nothing polymorphic survives:

define quad[#T] ( #T -- #T #T #T #T ) { twice twice twice }

define q ( i64 -- i64 i64 i64 i64 ) { quad }

locate q

\ That is the whole zero-cost story, and it is not a promise about an
\ optimizer. There is nowhere for a cost to hide: the residual is the program.

\ --- Generics call generics, at concrete types or at their own parameters.

define outer[#T] ( #T -- #T #T ) { twice[#T] }

9 outer show print " " print show print "\n" print

\ What one may NOT do is call itself, directly or through another. A call is
\ expanded where it stands, so a self-call asks to be expanded forever — and
\ this is caught by NAME, so it also catches mutual and polymorphic recursion.

define loopy[#T] ( #T -- #T ) { loopy[#T] }

1 loopy

\ ======================================================================
\ Writing the types out
\ ======================================================================

\ Matched types have to be pinned down by the INPUTS — the outputs are what
\ the call is trying to work out. A generic whose parameter appears only in
\ the output must be instantiated explicitly.

define mk0[#T] ( -- #T ) { 0 }

mk0

mk0[i64] show print "\n" print

\ Written types are CHECKED, not believed. They go to the same matcher, so the
\ stack must still agree and the body must still typecheck at them.

mk0[str]

1 twice[str]

\ ======================================================================
\ Linearity crosses generics with no extra rule
\ ======================================================================

\ `twice` is `{ dup }`, and `dup` needs its type to be `Copy`. `Box[t]` is
\ neither `Copy` nor `Drop` — it is linear — so `twice` is simply not
\ available at that type. The error comes from the body, checked at the call.

define boxy ( Box[i64] -- Box[i64] Box[i64] ) { twice }

\ ======================================================================
\ Generics compose with effects, and with the Dictionary
\ ======================================================================

effect Log { declare say ( str -- ) }

\ A generic body may perform operations. The row is part of the schema, so
\ every instance carries it.

define watched[#T] ( #T -- #T !Log ) { "  saw a value\n" say }

define use ( i64 -- i64 !Log ) { watched 1 + }

"(a) a generic that logs:\n" print
handle Log over ( i64 ) init { 0 } { say { swap pop 1 + } } { 41 use }
show print " message(s), result " print show print "\n" print

\ --- The code a generic splices in is ordinary code, so demo 03's rebinding
\ reaches into it. `bump` is called from inside an INSTANCE of `stepper`, and
\ the `with` at the call site still finds it.

define bump ( i64 -- i64 ) { 1 + }
define stepper[#T] ( #T i64 -- #T i64 ) { bump }
define big_bump ( i64 -- i64 ) { 1000 + }

define run ( -- str i64 ) { "tag" 0 stepper }
define run_big ( -- str i64 ) { with { bump big_bump } { "tag" 0 stepper } }

run swap pop show print " " print
run_big swap pop show print "\n" print

\ And the residuals show it happened at elaboration, not at run time: two
\ different programs, neither of which mentions the rebinding or the generic.

locate run

locate run_big

\ It reaches through NESTING too — an instance whose body instantiates another
\ generic, which calls the rebound word:

define twostep[#T] ( #T i64 -- #T i64 ) { stepper[#T] stepper[#T] }

define both ( -- str i64 ) { with { bump big_bump } { "tag" 0 twostep } }

locate both

\ --- Sharing. Identical instantiations are ELABORATED once and the result
\ reused, which is what keeps nesting from multiplying out: this chain would
\ otherwise build 3^4 copies of `twice`, and it returns immediately.

define g2[#T] ( #T -- #T #T #T #T ) { quad[#T] pop pop pop quad[#T] pop pop pop quad[#T] }
define g3[#T] ( #T -- #T #T #T #T ) { g2[#T] pop pop pop g2[#T] pop pop pop g2[#T] }

define deep ( i64 -- i64 i64 i64 i64 ) { g3[i64] }

locate deep

\ What is not yet shared is the emitted CODE — each call site carries its own
\ copy of the residual. The copies are now structurally equal, so what is
\ missing is a common-subexpression pass over the core rather than anything
\ about generics. Tracked as Q-19 in NOTES/N02_Open_Questions.md.
